explorations.core_result:
Every integral extreme point of the flow network corresponds to a deterministic allocation table \(a_i^t \in \{0,1\}\) satisfying:

- \(\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^t = H^t\) for each \(t\);  
- \(a_i^t = 0\) whenever \(v_i^t = 0\);  
- \(\sum_{k=1}^t a_i^k \in \{\lfloor V_i^t\rfloor, \lceil V_i^t\rceil\}\) for each \(i,t\).  

Because the network’s feasible region is an integral polytope and the fractional point \((v_i^t, V_i^t)\) is feasible, the fractional point is a convex combination of integral extreme points. The resulting probability distribution (measure of the convex combination) satisfies all required conditions and gives \(\mathbb{E}[a_i^t] = v_i^t\) for all \(i,t\). Hence the existence of the desired randomised offline allocation is proven.

explorations.found:
The step starts from the single‑source/single‑sink flow model that encodes the allocation problem, and transforms it into a pure circulation by adding an arc \(D \to S\) with lower bound = upper bound = \(L = \sum_{t=1}^T H^t\) (an integer). The nodes are: super‑source \(S\), super‑sink \(D\), time nodes \(T_t\) for \(t=1,\dots,T\), and agent‑cumulative nodes \(C_{i,0}\) (initial node) and \(C_{i,t}\) for \(i\in[n],\,t=1,\dots,T\). The arcs and their integer lower/upper bounds are:
- \(S \to T_t\): \((H^t, H^t)\);
- \(T_t \to C_{i,t}\) (only if \(v_i^t>0\)): \((0,1)\);
- \(C_{i,0} \to C_{i,1}\): \((0,0)\);
- \(C_{i,t} \to C_{i,t+1}\) for \(t=1,\dots,T-1\): \((\lfloor V_i^t\rfloor, \lceil V_i^t\rceil)\);
- \(C_{i,T} \to D\): \((\lfloor V_i^T\rfloor, \lceil V_i^T\rceil)\);
- \(D \to S\): \((L, L)\).

The fractional flow is set as:
- \(x_{S\to T_t}=H^t\);
- \(x_{T_t\to C_{i,t}}=v_i^t\) (when the arc exists);
- \(x_{C_{i,0}\to C_{i,1}}=0\);
- \(x_{C_{i,t}\to C_{i,t+1}}=V_i^t\) for \(t=1,\dots,T-1\);
- \(x_{C_{i,T}\to D}=V_i^T\);
- \(x_{D\to S}=L\).

Conservation at every node is verified: at \(S\), inflow \(L\) equals outflow \(\sum_t H^t = L\); at each \(T_t\), inflow \(H^t\) equals outflow \(\sum_i v_i^t = H^t\); at each \(C_{i,t}\) the inflow from the time arc and the previous cumulative arc sums to \(V_i^t\), matching the outflow; at \(D\) the inflow from all \(C_{i,T}\) is \(\sum_i V_i^T = \sum_t H^t = L\), matching outflow to \(S\). Hence the fractional data is a feasible circulation. All lower and upper bounds are integers by the problem’s hypotheses (\(H^t\) integer, floor/ceil of reals integers).

The directed graph’s node‑arc incidence matrix is totally unimodular. The feasible set of circulations is \(\{x \mid Ax = 0,\; l \le x \le u\}\) with integer right‑hand side zero, so by Hoffman’s circulation theorem the polytope is integral – every extreme point is integral. The fractional circulation is feasible, thus it can be expressed as a convex combination of integral extreme‑point circulations.

Mapping an integral circulation to a deterministic allocation table: each arc \(S\to T_t\) carries forced flow \(H^t\). The only outgoing arcs from \(T_t\) are the \(T_t\to C_{i,t}\) arcs, each with flow at most 1, so the number of arcs carrying flow 1 equals \(H^t\); define \(a_i^t\) as that flow. Then \(\sum_i a_i^t = H^t\), and if \(v_i^t=0\) the arc is absent, so \(a_i^t=0\). Flow conservation at \(C_{i,t}\) yields \(\text{flow}(C_{i,t}\to C_{i,t+1}) = \sum_{k=1}^t a_i^k\), and the bounds on that arc are \(\lfloor V_i^t\rfloor\) and \(\lceil V_i^t\rceil\); therefore \(\sum_{k=1}^t a_i^k \in \{\lfloor V_i^t\rfloor, \lceil V_i^t\rceil\}\). Similarly for \(t=T\). Thus each integral extreme point gives a feasible deterministic allocation.

Taking the convex combination of the integral extreme points that represent the fractional circulation yields a probability distribution over tables with marginals \(\mathbb{E}[a_i^t] = v_i^t\) (since the flow on each \(T_t\to C_{i,t}\) in the fractional circulation equals \(v_i^t\)), and all constraints hold with probability 1. The step concludes that the direction fully resolves the required existence proof.
